<Documentation>
  <p>Every effect accepts these creation options and returns the same <code>EffectHandle</code>. Effect-specific options belong to the <a href="/docs/api/effects">individual effect references</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="attachment-options">Creation options</h2>
  <DocsSharedOptions />
  <h2 id="control-playback">Writable controls</h2>
  <DocsEffectControls />
  <h2 id="animation-control">Control animation</h2>
  <p>Use <code>clock: 'auto'</code> for automatic animation. domfx advances effect time while it can render, without catching up time spent paused, hidden or offscreen. <code>'ambient'</code> is the older spelling of the same behavior and remains supported. The instance's read-only <code>clock</code> preserves the spelling you supplied.</p>
  <p>Use <code>clock: 'manual'</code> for external control through your code or GSAP. domfx never advances time in this mode, including after <code>resume()</code>. It still schedules rendering and observes the target; manual control does not mean immediate rendering.</p>
  <p><code>time</code> selects a point in the effect's animation in seconds. It is neither progress from 0 to 1 nor elapsed wall-clock time. You can seek backward or write negative values. Give one animation driver responsibility for each writable property.</p>
  <h2 id="reduced-motion">Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>The default <code>motion: 'respect'</code> suppresses output when the user requests reduced motion. After the preference changes back, explicitly call <code>retry()</code>. It does not automatically replace animation with a still frame.</p>
  <p><code>motion: 'static'</code> always renders a still effect at time zero, under either preference. Time writes remain readable but do not change its rendered frame. Intensity and capture updates still work. <code>resume()</code> does not animate it.</p>
  <p>See <a href="/docs/api/effect-handle">effect state and methods</a> for readiness, pause/resume, refresh, retry and cleanup.</p>
</Documentation>
